            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

The results of the blower door air leakage test indicate that your house is quite airtight currently and would include a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit if it was being constructed today. Although your house is quite airtight, there are still some nuisance locations that need to be addressed:



** The fireplace on the main floor does not appear to have a damper (I think it is broken &amp; has been removed)



** The location of the lower level fireplace box (up behind/around the visible clay chimney pipe)



** Some of the exterior wall plugs &amp; switches



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate you will easily achieve a 20% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 212 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.8 GJ, and qualify for a $ 750 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade. Even if you don't proceed with a wall insulation upgrade, addressing the air leakage associated with the current wood fireplace &amp; the chimney box location of the lower level should still result in 15% ($159 annual) heat loss savings.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior perimeter walls of your house appear to be solid 4" thick (on average) timbers assembled vertically for the full combined height of the lower basement &amp; upper main floor. These walls have a tar weather/moisture barrier on the exterior of the in-ground portion of the foundation walls and a mesh/stucco on the exterior of the above grade walls. The interior of the walls appears to have about 2.25" (on average) of horizontal strapping (with about R7 fibreglass batt) that supports the interior drywall surface. With the combined R-value of the insulation and the solid wood, there is about R11 of total effective wall insulation - similar to what is accomplished by a 2x4 R12 wall constructed in Fort Smith back in the 70's.



We did talk about the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP)  that was launched on April 1, 2019 to see if this may be something you would be interested in. I have modeled adding another R15 to all of the exterior walls, including the wall that adjoins the garage. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings: By adding R15 to all of the exterior, you could potentially save $ 521 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the DHERP. As your house is generally quite air tight &amp; it has a pretty efficient oil boiler ........... you will likely find that adding more wall insulation would only be cost-effective for you if you were already considering a full exterior renovation to the house.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

There are some windows already upgraded in the house, and we talked about the efficiency gains if you were to upgrade the remaining 10 windows along the rear/left basement walls &amp; the wood windows remaining on the main floor. Please note the bay window in considered one total window unit.



I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading these 10 older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing these 10 older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 369 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.9 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window in rebates (12 windows maximum) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 184 litre electric hot water tank. We talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 622 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a Biasi B10/4 oil boiler (87% efficiency). If you were to consider having an on-demand propane hot water heater, I thought I would also model an upgrade from the oil boiler  to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 165 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 17.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. If you completed an eligible wall insulation upgrade through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, this rebate would be doubled to $1,500.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has three toilets and two of them (in your master bathroom &amp; the one in the basement) are older inefficient models, and you should consider replacing them with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Wood Stove Fireplace Insert



The current wood fireplace upstairs has a broken/missing damper and open fireplaces (even with glass doors) are not very efficient. I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency boiler &amp; the cost of wood in Fort Smith at the $100/cord "self-harvested" rate) if you were to install a new wood stove fireplace insert, used for about 75% of the heating requirement for the upper floor of the house.



I modeled an upgrade to a new EPA qualified woodstove (a fireplace insert) that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe and an efficiency of at least 80%. Wood stoves must meet either the Canadian Standards Association (CSA) B415.1-M92 (performance testing of solid-fuel burning appliances) or the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) wood burning appliances standards (40 CFR Part 60).



Rebates &amp; Savings: By using an 80% efficient wood stove for 75% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house, you could potentially save $ 2,514 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% efficient propane boiler. Installing an eligible wood stove qualifies for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs and fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a range hood fan   

        - two older bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



NOTE: Overall your house is quite air tight &amp; will become even more air tight when the broken/missing damper in the fireplace is fixed. If your house was being built today it would include a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) to manage indoor air quality and provide adequate ventilation. As it is hard to retrofit in an HRV into a house heated by a boiler and has a fully finished basement, some additional ventilation may be provided by using a main floor bathroom exhaust fan that operates via a humidistat or a programmable timer. If you find the indoor air quality becoming poor, or there is excess condensation forming along the bottom glass of the windows, you may want to consider having the main bathroom fan set up as a "principal ventilation fan".



You should consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seals well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood, both bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.4 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current wood fireplace vents up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see some older smoke detectors and one plug-in carbon monoxide (CO) detector. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.25 per kWh

Oil  - $1.17 per litre 

Propane - $0.87 per litre

Wood - $242 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $8.49 per bag, $288.46 per tonne bulk delivered



